<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!--
      THE SECOND SERVICE. Day-07.

      The aggregator's comment said "later days add siblings here, each its own
      folder + its own pom". This is that day, and the sibling is where the
      architecture stops being a claim and starts costing something.
    -->
    <parent>
        <groupId>com.persona</groupId>
        <artifactId>persona-microservices</artifactId>
        <version>0.0.1-SNAPSHOT</version>
        <relativePath>../../pom.xml</relativePath>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>wallet-service</artifactId>
    <name>wallet-service</name>
    <description>Owns everything about a persona wallet: balance, status, money movement</description>

    <!--
      ===================================================================
      WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND WHY IT IS THE MOST IMPORTANT PART OF THIS FILE
      ===================================================================

      There is NO dependency on user-service. None. Not a shared jar, not a
      "persona-common" module with the User class in it, nothing.

      That is the single decision that makes this a microservice rather than
      a distributed monolith, and it is worth being concrete about what it
      forbids:

        - wallet-service cannot `import com.persona.user.model.User`
        - it cannot write `@OneToOne` to a User
        - its migration cannot write `REFERENCES users(id)`
        - it cannot join wallets to users in SQL
        - it cannot participate in a transaction that also writes a user

      Every one of those is available in the monolith, and all five are given
      up together. They are not five decisions; they are one.

      THE TEMPTING SHORTCUT, named so it can be refused on purpose: extract a
      `persona-common` module holding User, Wallet and the shared DTOs, and
      depend on it from both services. It compiles, it removes duplication,
      and it quietly re-couples the two services at build time — now a change
      to User forces a rebuild and redeploy of wallet-service, which is
      exactly the property microservices exist to avoid. Shared DTOs are the
      most common way a microservice system becomes a monolith with network
      calls in the middle.

      So wallet-service knows a user only as a `Long userId`. It cannot
      validate that the id refers to anybody. See V1's comment for what that
      costs and what replaces it.
      ===================================================================
    -->
    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-webmvc</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-webmvc-test</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          Validation, and the user-service pom's comment on this dependency is
          worth re-reading here because today it stops being hypothetical:

            "In a microservice the natural next step is that this service
             passes the value to ANOTHER service, and the failure surfaces
             over there — in a log nobody watching this deployment is reading,
             attributed to the wrong team."

          A deposit amount arrives here from user-service, not from a browser.
          It is still validated here. A service does not get to assume its
          caller validated, even when its caller is also you.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          Security + jjwt, same three-artifact split as user-service.

          WHY A SECOND SERVICE NEEDS ITS OWN JWT VALIDATION. The token is
          issued by user-service and presented here. wallet-service does not
          call user-service to ask "is this token real?" — it verifies the
          signature itself, with the same shared secret, and that is the
          entire appeal of a stateless token: authentication scales to N
          services without N calls back to the issuer.

          The cost is that the SECRET is now in two deployments. Rotating it
          means rotating both, together, or every token minted by one is
          rejected by the other. That shared secret is the coupling a
          public/private key pair (RS256) would remove, which is a later
          refinement and is named here so the trade is visible rather than
          invisible.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.jsonwebtoken</groupId>
            <artifactId>jjwt-api</artifactId>
            <version>0.12.6</version>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.jsonwebtoken</groupId>
            <artifactId>jjwt-impl</artifactId>
            <version>0.12.6</version>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>io.jsonwebtoken</groupId>
            <artifactId>jjwt-jackson</artifactId>
            <version>0.12.6</version>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          JPA. Identical to user-service's, and that pom's comment on this
          dependency predicted today exactly:

            "in a microservice, an @Entity may only describe tables THIS
             service owns. The moment a Wallet entity here holds a @ManyToOne
             to a User owned by another service, the object graph crosses a
             database boundary that does not exist — and JPA will happily let
             you write it. The annotation cannot tell you that your
             architecture is wrong."

          Compare com.persona.wallet.model.Wallet in this service against
          com.persona.model.Wallet in the monolith. Same class, same job; one
          has @OneToOne User and one has a bare Long. That diff IS Day-07.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
            <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>

        <!--
          Flyway — AND ITS OWN MIGRATION HISTORY.

          This service's migrations start at V1 even though user-service also
          has a V1. They are different databases, so they have different
          flyway_schema_history tables and the version numbers are independent.

          Worth internalising, because it is the schema-level statement of the
          whole architecture: there is no global "version 4 of persona". Each
          service versions its own schema, and the deployment order between
          them is a thing humans have to reason about rather than a thing
          Flyway can enforce.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
            <artifactId>flyway-core</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
            <artifactId>flyway-database-postgresql</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <!-- Boot 4 split: FlywayAutoConfiguration ships here, not in
             spring-boot-autoconfigure. Without it Flyway is on the classpath,
             silent, and never runs. See user-service's pom for how that was
             found the hard way. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-flyway</artifactId>
        </dependency>

    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <configuration>
                    <includes>
                        <include>**/*Test.java</include>
                        <include>**/*IT.java</include>
                    </includes>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
